  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1153 AM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nWith light and variable winds and clear skies in store for Thursday \nnight into Friday morning, overnight lows likely to be the cooler\nones of this week. We are forecasting lows in the mid to upper \n40s and low 50s.\n\nFriday is looking like another excellent day with partly cloudy to \nsunny skies and highs in the low to upper 70s range. As the night \nprogresses, a weather pattern change will be on the way. An upper \nlevel wave is forecast to push across northwest Mexico and spreads \nto the east that night through Saturday and exiting South Central \nTexas on Sunday. Scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms are \nforecast to begin early Saturday morning out west along the Rio \nGrande and slowly moving eastward throughout the day. The RRFS, \nGEFS, and EPS have a good handle as far as the passage of upper \nlevel trough axis over the Rio Grande by Saturday morning. After \nthat, the ensembles differ somewhat with a new upper level short \nwave moving across the Four Corners region into the Southern \nPlains and into east Texas. With that said, there are \nuncertainties for this coming weekend as far as precipitation and \nfor how long is going to last. We do know about the potential for \nrain and the possibility for flooding across parts of South \nCentral Texas for the weekend especially on Saturday. WPC Day 4 \nERO highlights with level 1 of 4 for risk of rainfall exceeding \nflash flood guidance and for the Day 5 ERO areas to the east of \nHighway 281. Definitely something to monitor closely in the \ncoming days leading to the weekend. By Sunday afternoon into the \nevening, a cold front is forecast to push across the local area \nwith the last round of showers and storms over the eastern portion\nof South Central Texas. Dry and cool weather is favored for \nMonday through Wednesday of next week.",
